## Runtime

The system runs entirely on consumer hardware and has gone through three major architectures.

- **v1 — Mistral 7B:** early local-inference prototype
- **v2 — Qwen3 30B:** Windows handled speech/orchestration while an M4 MacBook Pro handled LLM inference through oMLX
- **v3 — Qwen3.8 27B:** inference moved to the PC GPU; the Mac now handles Pocket-TTS and shared runtime services

The diagram shows the **v2 architecture**.

The runtime work eventually became its own project: `Hibi`, a supervisor for starting, stopping, and monitoring the local services underneath ALL-LLLM Pod and other applications.

## Transcript Intelligence

The source transcripts became a substantial project of their own.

WhisperX provides canonical words and timestamps. NeMo performs speaker attribution inside those boundaries, while human-in-the-loop tooling is used to inspect ambiguous segments and repair errors.

That pipeline ultimately produced more than 75,000 usable transcript segments for persona modeling.

## Ghost Audio

While auditing Episode 136, I found speech that was clearly audible but absent from every generated transcript.

That led to a decoder-level investigation into what I called **Ghost Audio**: short utterances that disappear depending on decoding configuration.

I built BERD-1 to compare alternate Whisper decoding paths and surface likely missing segments for review. Most of the recovered candidates were short interjections and disfluencies — exactly the kind of speech that matters when trying to reproduce how a person actually talks.

## Why build it

ALL-LLLM Pod started as a parody.

It became a practical way to explore a harder question: how much engineering sits underneath a conversational AI persona that feels coherent across speech recognition, language generation, voice, timing, memory, and interaction?
